<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qatif</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qatif is an oasis town and port on the Gulf coast of eastern Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> islands. Long the main harbour of the al-Ahsa region, it was held in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, Uyunids, Jarwanids and Jabrids, and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the Banu Khalid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> described its Shi'a Arab population around 1331, and it remains a centre of Saudi Arabia's Shi'a community in the oil-rich Eastern Province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qatif.jpg" alt="Qatif" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atheer Al-Sadah · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qatif_water_tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Qatif is a green oasis with rich agricultural soil, surrounded by palm groves and fed by many springs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> lies to the east, with warm, calm waters rich in fish, shrimp and pearl oysters, and the ad-Dahna Desert to the west. The historic oasis shows archaeological evidence of settlement from about 3500 BCE. The name Qatif is derived from a word meaning 'harvest' or 'grain', and an older name, Al-Khatt, appears in the poetry of Antarah ibn Shaddad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarafa">Tarafa</a> ibn Al-'Abd. Khatty became a preferred poetic word for 'spear', supposedly because the region was known for making them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Qatif functioned for centuries as the most important trade port of Eastern Arabia, so that the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> was called the 'Sea of Qatif'. It belonged to the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> and the Bahrain islands. In 899 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> conquered Qatif and al-Hasa and ruled until 1071. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> raided Qatif in 988, and in 1059 it was freed from Qarmatian rule in the Battle of Qatif. The Uyunids held the region from 1071 until 1253, when the Usfurids took over. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1331 and found a large, prosperous city. Power passed to the Jabrids of al-Hasa in 1440.</p><p>In 1520 the Portuguese sacked Qatif and killed the Jabrid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqrin-ibn-zamil">Muqrin ibn Zamil</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took over in 1549, and the Portuguese conquered Qatif in 1551. The Banu Khalid ruled the region until 1790, when the First Saudi State took it, and the Second Saudi State held all of it from 1830. The Ottomans returned in 1871 and remained until 1913, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> established Saudi rule in the Eastern Province. Qatif Municipality was founded in 1926. In the 1920s the economy declined, first through the invention of cultured pearls, which hurt the Gulf pearl trade, and then through heavy taxes. After oil fields were developed in the late 1940s Qatif lost its status as an important port to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dammam">Dammam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Qatif is a governorate of the Eastern Province of Saudi Arabia, covering about 481 square kilometres, with Qatif City as its seat. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> it forms the historical core of the province, and it is the centre of the Shia population of Saudi Arabia. Most people are Arab Baharna, with a bedouin minority and some Sunni Gulf Arabs. The oasis keeps old villages such as Al-Awamia and Al-Qala'a and Tarout Castle, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> season and the children's custom of Qarqi'an are marked each year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qatif” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1670 their chief Barrak ibn Ghurayr expelled the Ottoman garrison of the Lahsa Eyalet and made Hofuf the centre of an emirate controlling the coast from Kuwait to Qatar and the oases of al-Hasa and <b>Qatif</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khalid-emirate">Banu Khalid Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The state taxed oasis farmers and tribes and drew on Gulf trade through al-Hasa and <b>Qatif</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd">Emirate of Nejd</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1440 they took al-Ahsa and then <b>Qatif</b> and the Bahrain islands from the Jarwanid dynasty, establishing a Sunni dynasty based in al-Ahsa.</blockquote><a href="/state/jabrid-dynasty">Jabrid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jarwanids took control of <b>Qatif</b> and the Bahrain islands in the early 14th century, displacing the Usfurid dynasty there.</blockquote><a href="/state/jarwanid-dynasty">Jarwanid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The province of Lahsa was organised around 1560 with its seat at Hofuf and included the port of <b>Qatif</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lahsa-eyalet">Lahsa Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>In 899 the missionary Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi, preaching the coming of the Mahdi, won over Bedouin and peasants in eastern Arabia, then called Bahrain, and took al-Ahsa and <b>Qatif</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarmatians-of-bahrain">Qarmatians of Bahrain</a></li></ul></section>
